DOSBox: a rhythm-mode drum is panned by its own channel

Channel::GeneratePercussion() sums the five drums of rhythm mode into one sample and adds that to both outputs. Every other sound of this core leaves through (sample * panLeft / 65535) & maskLeft and its right-hand twin: the two bits of the C0 register, which are how a YMF262 says which output a channel is heard on, and the soft panning libADLMIDI added beside them. The drums leave through neither. A note at the top of the file has said so since DOSBox wrote it -- "Fix panning for the Percussion channels, would any opl3 player use it and actually really change it though?" -- and the answer to the question it ends with is yes.

A player does use it, this one among them. OPL3::setPan() writes the panning bits of whichever chip channel a note is playing on, and in rhythm mode those are channels 6, 7 and 8; a drum of a bank that asks for rhythm mode is played there, and the controller that pans its part is written to the chip and then dropped on the floor. The other cores here do not drop it. Nuked OPL3, which is written from a die scan of the chip, mixes channels 6, 7 and 8 like all the others, each through its own cha/chb and chl/chr.

The measurement, a rhythm-mode bass drum, snare drum and tom-tom keyed on one at a time, peak of the left output and peak of the right, in this core and in Nuked OPL3. With both bits set and panning at centre: 8144 and 8144 here, 5781 and 5781 there. With only the left bit set: 8144 and 8144 here, 5781 and 0 there. With both bits set and soft panning hard left: 8144 and 8144 here, 8178 and 0 there. So the drums were heard from both outputs whatever was asked for, and they were also louder than everything else by the square root of two, which is the pan law's attenuation at centre that every other sound of the core is given and these were not.

The drums are summed by the channel whose operators make them now -- the bass drum on this channel, the hi-hat and the snare drum on the next, the tom-tom and the top cymbal on the one after, which is where the chip has them -- and each sum leaves through that channel's panning. The same measurement after: 5758 and 5758, 5758 and 0, 8144 and 0, which is Nuked OPL3's pattern to the sample the two cores differ by. OPL2 mode is untouched: there is no panning there, and the sum goes out as it did. The note at the top of the file goes with the fault it named.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is upstream.

diff --git a/src/chips/dosbox/dbopl.cpp b/src/chips/dosbox/dbopl.cpp
--- a/src/chips/dosbox/dbopl.cpp
+++ b/src/chips/dosbox/dbopl.cpp
@@ -26,7 +26,6 @@
 
 	//TODO Don't delay first operator 1 sample in opl3 mode
 	//TODO Maybe not use class method pointers but a regular function pointers with operator as first parameter
-	//TODO Fix panning for the Percussion channels, would any opl3 player use it and actually really change it though?
 	//TODO Check if having the same accuracy in all frequency multipliers sounds better or not
 
 	//DUNNO Keyon in 4op, switch to 2op without keyoff.
@@ -861,7 +860,7 @@ template< bool opl3Mode>
 INLINE void Channel::GeneratePercussion( Chip* chip, Bit32s* output ) {
 	Channel* chan = this;
 
-	//BassDrum
+	//BassDrum, made by the operators of this channel
 	Bit32s mod = (Bit32u)((old[0] + old[1])) >> feedback;
 	old[0] = old[1];
 	old[1] = static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(0)->GetSample( mod ));
@@ -881,34 +880,46 @@ INLINE void Channel::GeneratePercussion( Chip* chip, Bit32s* output ) {
 	Bit32u c5 = static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(5)->ForwardWave());
 	Bit32u phaseBit = (((c2 & 0x88) ^ ((c2<<5) & 0x80)) | ((c5 ^ (c5<<2)) & 0x20)) ? 0x02 : 0x00;
 
-	//Hi-Hat
+	//Hi-Hat, made by an operator of the channel after this one
+	Bit32s sampleNext = 0;
 	Bit32u hhVol = static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(2)->ForwardVolume());
 	if ( !ENV_SILENT( hhVol ) ) {
 		Bit32u hhIndex = (phaseBit<<8) | (0x34 << ( phaseBit ^ (noiseBit << 1 )));
-		sample += static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(2)->GetWave( hhIndex, hhVol ));
+		sampleNext += static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(2)->GetWave( hhIndex, hhVol ));
 	}
-	//Snare Drum
+	//Snare Drum, by the other operator of that channel
 	Bit32u sdVol = static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(3)->ForwardVolume());
 	if ( !ENV_SILENT( sdVol ) ) {
 		Bit32u sdIndex = ( 0x100 + (c2 & 0x100) ) ^ ( noiseBit << 8 );
-		sample += static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(3)->GetWave( sdIndex, sdVol ));
+		sampleNext += static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(3)->GetWave( sdIndex, sdVol ));
 	}
-	//Tom-tom
-	sample += static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(4)->GetSample( 0 ));
+	//Tom-tom, by an operator of the channel after that
+	Bit32s sampleLast = static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(4)->GetSample( 0 ));
 
-	//Top-Cymbal
+	//Top-Cymbal, by the other operator of that channel
 	Bit32u tcVol = static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(5)->ForwardVolume());
 	if ( !ENV_SILENT( tcVol ) ) {
 		Bit32u tcIndex = (1 + phaseBit) << 8;
-		sample += static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(5)->GetWave( tcIndex, tcVol ));
+		sampleLast += static_cast<Bit32u>(Op(5)->GetWave( tcIndex, tcVol ));
 	}
 	sample <<= 1;
-	// if ( opl3Mode ) {
-	output[0] += sample;
-	output[1] += sample;
-	// } else {
-	// 	output[0] += sample;
-	// }
+	sampleNext <<= 1;
+	sampleLast <<= 1;
+	if ( opl3Mode ) {
+		//Each drum is panned by the channel whose operators make it, the way
+		//every other sound of the chip is panned by its own channel.
+		const Channel* next = this + 1;
+		const Channel* last = this + 2;
+		output[0] += ((sample * panLeft / 65535) & maskLeft)
+			+ ((sampleNext * next->panLeft / 65535) & next->maskLeft)
+			+ ((sampleLast * last->panLeft / 65535) & last->maskLeft);
+		output[1] += ((sample * panRight / 65535) & maskRight)
+			+ ((sampleNext * next->panRight / 65535) & next->maskRight)
+			+ ((sampleLast * last->panRight / 65535) & last->maskRight);
+	} else {
+		output[0] += sample + sampleNext + sampleLast;
+		output[1] += sample + sampleNext + sampleLast;
+	}
 }
 
 template<SynthMode mode>
